<Guide
  title="How to Get Your Business Recommended by ChatGPT & Google AI — Azoth & Co."
  description="What ChatGPT, Perplexity and Google’s AI answers read before they recommend a local business, and the seven fixes that get you named. A plain guide for owners in Dallas–Fort Worth."
  label="AI search"
  heading="How to get recommended by ChatGPT and Google’s AI answers."
  sub="Customers now ask an assistant who to call. Here is what it reads before it answers, and how to be on the list."
  published="2026-11-11"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    Ask ChatGPT for the best med spa in McKinney or a reliable plumber in Frisco and it will name two or three
    businesses. It does not pick them at random, and it does not take money to pick them. It names the ones it can
    confirm.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the whole game. An assistant is cautious by design. It recommends a business when several sources agree
    that the business exists, does what the customer asked, works where the customer is, and is well regarded. A
    business that is excellent but thinly documented gets left out.
  </p>

  <h2>What the assistants read</h2>
  <p>
    The sources vary by assistant and change often, but the pattern is steady. Before naming a local business, AI
    answers lean on:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Search results.</strong> ChatGPT and Perplexity search the web live. Google’s AI answers draw on Google’s own index and your Business Profile. If you do not rank, you are less likely to be read.</li>
    <li><strong>Your Google Business Profile.</strong> Categories, services, hours, reviews and your replies.</li>
    <li><strong>Reviews in more than one place.</strong> Google, Yelp, Facebook and the directories that matter in your trade.</li>
    <li><strong>Directories and listings.</strong> Consistent name, address and phone across the sites customers and data providers use.</li>
    <li><strong>Your own website.</strong> Clear service pages that say what you do and where, in plain words a machine can quote.</li>
    <li><strong>What others say.</strong> Local news, chamber listings, suppliers, Reddit and community threads.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Seven fixes, in order</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Make your Business Profile complete and exact.</strong> The right primary category, every service you
      offer, your real service area, hours, photos. This is the single biggest lever for Google’s map and its AI
      answers. Our <a href="/visibility/google-business-profile/">Business Profile checklist</a> covers it field by field.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Give every core service its own page.</strong> One page for “Botox”, one for “HydraFacial”, one for “AC
      repair”. Say what it is, who it is for, what it costs or how it is priced, and which towns you serve. An assistant
      cannot recommend you for a service your site only mentions in a list.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Write answers, not slogans.</strong> Assistants quote clear sentences. “We repair and replace residential
      AC systems in McKinney, Allen and Frisco, usually same day” is quotable. “Comfort you can count on” is not.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fix your name, address and phone everywhere.</strong> Mismatched listings make a business look unsure of
      itself. Check the major directories and your trade’s own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ask every happy customer for a review, and reply to all of them.</strong> Recent, specific reviews that
      mention the service and the town carry the most weight. Replies show a business that is present.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Get mentioned off your own site.</strong> A chamber listing, a local feature, a supplier’s dealer page, a
      genuine answer in a community thread. Each is another source that agrees you exist.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Add structured data.</strong> LocalBusiness and FAQ markup tells machines, in their own language, what you
      do, where, and when you are open.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What not to do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Do not post fake reviews or pay for them. Platforms remove them, and the FTC fines for them.</li>
    <li>Do not stuff city names into your business name on Google. It breaks Google’s rules and can get the profile suspended.</li>
    <li>Do not build dozens of near-identical city pages. Search engines treat them as doorway pages.</li>
    <li>Do not trust anyone who guarantees AI placement. Nobody controls it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to measure it</h2>
  <p>
    Keep a short list of the questions your customers really ask. Once a month, ask them in ChatGPT, Perplexity and
    Google. Note who gets named and which sources are cited. Pair that with your Business Profile calls and direction
    requests, and your site’s Search Console data. That is enough to see whether the work is moving.
  </p>
</Guide>
